## Arithmetic, one token at a time

Ask a model to multiply two four-digit numbers and it writes the answer the only way it can: one
likely-looking token after another (Ch 16). Sometimes that works. Often it doesn't. Now give it a
calculator:

<ToolsDemo />

Without tools, the model wrote out a long multiplication and got 7,050,952. The right answer is
7,006,652. With a calculator, it got it right, because it never did the arithmetic at all.

## A tool call is just text

The model can't press buttons or run programs. A <GlossaryPopover term="tool-call">tool call</GlossaryPopover> is text the model writes in an agreed
format, like the answer line in Ch 21, but for the harness to act on. Models can be trained to write calls like this at the right moments.<Cite id="schick2023" />
Then:

1. The harness watches the model's output for that format and stops it there.
2. It reads out the tool's name and arguments, and runs the tool itself.
3. It adds the result to the conversation and calls the model again.

Every model family has its own format. Qwen writes a small block of JSON between `<tool_call>` tags.
On this site, calls are shown in a simpler made-up form, like
`<call calculator>1234 * 5678</call>`, so they're easy to read.

The tools in these chapters are toys that run in your browser: a calculator, a search over the six
nursery rhymes from Ch 1, a word counter, a fake web page, and a pretend file list. Real assistants
use the same pattern for web search, code execution, sending email, and more, which is exactly why
the harness has to be careful about which tools it runs.

<RecapCard>

- Models are unreliable at exact work like arithmetic, because every digit is a predicted token.
- A tool call is text in an agreed format. The model asks; the harness runs the tool.
- The harness adds the result to the prompt and calls the model again.

</RecapCard>

**Next question:** one tool call helps. What if a task needs several, one after another?
